  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 2: Measure the channel, don't trust its datasheet"
            prompt="The transmitter is confirmed healthy. Before anyone starts tuning an equalizer, characterize the channel itself: add a Lossy Channel between the generator and the Eye Scope and see how closed the eye actually gets. The cable assembly's datasheet lists about 11 dB of insertion loss at Nyquist for a run this length - is that actually what you're measuring?"
            hint="Check the Lossy Channel editor's own &quot;loss&quot; setting against the 11 dB the datasheet claims. Does the as-built part match its own paperwork?"
            explanation="The channel is actually configured for 16 dB of loss at Nyquist, not the 11 dB its datasheet claims for this length - a real &quot;the as-built part doesn't match its own paperwork&quot; finding (a different cable gauge, an uncounted connector, extra board loss nobody budgeted for). With zero equalization anywhere in the chain the eye is almost completely shut - around 2% height, under a fifth of a UI wide - exactly what 16 dB alone does at this rate. The number that matters for every later step is the one you just measured, 16 dB, not the 11 dB on the datasheet: sizing the TX FFE for the wrong number is exactly the mistake Bring-Up 3 walks into."/>
</LABCHAIN>
